 # Splatting
-Also called: Object-order splatting, footprint rendering, kernel-based volume rendering
-
 ## Overview
-Splatting is an object-order volume rendering technique that computes the contribution of each voxel to the image by convolving the voxel with a filter (kernel) that distributes its value to a neighborhood of pixels on the screen [S1, S2]. Unlike ray-casting, which determines how every voxel affects a specific pixel (image-order), splatting determines how every voxel affects every pixel it covers (object-order) [S3]. It is distinguished from siblings like [Shear-warp](24-shear-warp.md) and [Texture slicing](25-texture-slicing.md) by its use of continuous, overlapping footprints (splats) rather than discrete slice projections, offering rendering quality similar to ray casting but generally slower than shear-warp [S1].
+Splatting is an object-order volume rendering technique that computes the contribution of each voxel to the image by convolving the voxel with a filter (kernel) that distributes its value to a neighborhood of pixels on the screen [S1, S2]. Unlike ray-casting, which determines how every voxel affects a specific pixel (image-order), splatting determines how every voxel affects every pixel it covers (object-order) [S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from siblings like [Shear-warp](24-shear-warp.md) and [Texture slicing](25-texture-slicing.md) by its use of continuous, overlapping footprints (splats) rather than discrete slice projections, offering rendering quality similar to ray casting but generally slower than shear-warp [S1].
 
