+### Extinction-based splatting
 A rendering solution introduced in [S4] that uses extinction (referred to as 'density' in NeRF literature) instead of the learned opacity value used in 3DGS, to analyze the difference between opacity-based and accurate volumetric rendering. For small numbers of primitives, extinction-based splatting performs better than opacity-based splatting. However, with more primitives, opacity splatting performs best [S4].
 
-#### Perspective-Correct Point Splatting Vertex Shader
-A vertex shader that renders point clouds as splats (quads or points) with size attenuation based on perspective projection, ensuring the visual size of points corresponds to their world-space radius [S5]. It transforms the input position `in_position` to clip space using the `view_proj_matrix` and assigns to `gl_Position` [S5]. It calculates `gl_PointSize` using the formula: `viewport_size[1] * (1 / tan(fov_y / 2)) * particle_radius / gl_Position.w`, scaling the point size inversely with depth (`gl_Position.w`) [S5]. It passes the `particle_radius` and `in_color` to the fragment shader via the output struct `VertOut` [S5]. Parameters include `fov_y` (vertical field of view in radians), `viewport_size` (viewport dimensions, specifically height), and `particle_radius` (world-space radius) [S5].
