-#### Splatting Without the Blur
-A volume rendering algorithm that performs classification and shading after voxels are projected onto the screen to avoid blurring object edges, which occurs when classification is done prior to projection [S6]. It projects voxel densities onto the screen using regular splats (radially symmetric interpolation kernels) [S6]. It projects gradient volumes onto the screen using gradient splats, OR computes gradients on the projection plane using central differencing on the projected density [S6]. It performs per-pixel shading using the computed gradients to classify and color the voxels [S6]. An optimization uses lookup tables for shading to handle the added overhead of screen gradients and per-pixel shading [S6]. Computing gradients on the projection plane via central differencing reduces the number of footprint rasterizations by a factor of four compared to projecting a separate gradient volume, because only voxel densities need to be projected [S6].
